// The boss journal: Morrow-Oak, the Starved Grove, the oldest tree in the realm, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Morrow-Oak has no spoken lines in the journal; the heroes speak for him. Move keys are cast names (the journal fires on the cast bar's name).
// The idea every page carries: what the Hunger eats stays gone, so the party chooses where the holes go (out at the edge, never the middle), and a hole is a fall, not a death.
// The Grove Remade covers his second form and the first look at the four limbs; the limbs' own moves have their own pages. Deeper Roots is the Hard name of his unfolding.
// Twin Seed, Hungering Dark, Deeper Roots, Wyrm Wing, Frost Fist, Archmage Rune, Hydra Head and Last Gasp are the Hard-only pages.
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).morrowoak = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "An elder tree as tall as a tower, his heartwood split open on a dark with no stars in it. What that dark eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the ground is gone for good, so it is walked to the edge. A hole is a fall. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "A tree tall as a tower, split down the heart on a dark with no stars. What it swallows stays gone. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is lost, so it is carried to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow, not a death. I know the sensation.",
    thresher: "Such a tall old fellow, dear, and a great split in his heart where the stars forgot to go. Whatever that dark eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and wherever it bursts the floor is gone, so we carry it to the edge. A fall is only a fall. Not yet!",
    bloodreaver: "The big one is a tree the size of a tower with a black wound for a heart! Whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so we carry it to the edge. A fall hurts, but it doesn't kill. A fine scar.",
    runeguard: "The omen: a tree as tall as a tower, his heartwood split on a dark without stars, and a seed that rides one of us. The answer: carry it to the edge, for wherever it bursts the floor is gone for good. A hole is a fall, not a death. As foreseen.",
    gorgemaw: "My host is a tree like a tower, with a split heart that eats and never says grace. Whatever it takes stays taken. A seed rides one of my friends and bursts into a hole, so we carry it to the rim, where the loss is least. A fall is a bruise. Promising.",
    oathbroker: "The counterparty is an elder tree, tall as a tower, his heartwood split upon a dark without stars. The fine print: what it eats is not returned. A seed rides one of the undersigned, and where it bursts the floor is forfeit, so it goes to the edge. A fall is a penalty. So noted.",
    ironpalm: "He is a tree as tall as a tower, split down the heart on a dark with no stars. What the dark eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us. We carry it to the edge, and let it open there. A fall is a heavy word, not the last one. Breathe.",
    ranger: "Tree, tall as a tower, heart split open on a dark with no stars in it. Seed rides one of us, and where it bursts, the floor's gone for good. Carry it to the edge. A hole is a fall, not a grave. Clean line, if you place it.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is a tower of a tree with a black split for a heart, and the dark in it eats. It plants a seed on one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone. Carry it to the edge. A fall is a bruise, not a burial. It never saw me.",
    pyromancer: "A tree as tall as a tower! I was so pleased, until I saw its heart: a split full of starless dark that eats whatever falls in. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone forever, so we carry it to the edge. A fall hurts. Mostly unsinged.",
    thermist: "Cold read: a tree tall as a tower, a split heart, a dark with no stars that eats. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Carry it to the edge. Hot read: I wanted to run. A fall is only a fall. Back to the middle.",
    driftblade: "He's a tree tall as a tower with a black split for a heart, and a seed lands on one of us, and wherever it bursts the floor is gone for good, so we carry it out through everyone to the edge and let it open there, and falling is only a fall.",
    alembist: "Specimen: a tree tall as a tower, heart split open on a dark with no stars. Observed: whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Note: carry it to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow, not a death.",
    threadweaver: "A tower of a tree with a split heart full of starless dark that eats and never gives back. A seed rides one of my stitches, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so we pull it out to the edge, away from the rest. A fall is a snag. Mind the slack.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man is a tree like a tower with a black split for a heart, and the dark in it eats the stage itself. A seed rides one of the company, and where it bursts the boards are gone for good. Carry it to the wings. (A fall is a bruise, not a curtain.)",
    cleric: "Poor old tree, tall as a tower, his heart split open on a dark with no stars, and it eats. A seed rides one of my dear ones, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good, so we carry it to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow. Dawn keep us.",
    grovekeeper: "Oh, he was an oak once, my kin, tall as a tower, and now a split in his heart full of starless dark that eats. A seed rides one of my lot, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Carry it to the edge. A fall is a bruise. Plant first.",
    chronomancer: "A tree tall as a tower, heart split on a dark with no stars, and whatever it eats stays eaten, even for me. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Carry it to the edge; I will have said so. A fall is not an undoing.",
    tidecaller: "A tree as tall as a tower, split down the heart on a dark that eats like an undertow and gives nothing back. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so we carried it to the edge. A fall is a rip, not a drowning. Mind the tide.",
    lanternkeeper: "A tree as tall as a tower, a split in his heart where no light has ever been. Whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone, so I walked with them to the edge. A fall is a heavy blow. Keep your light.",
    kenseier: "A tree tall as a tower, a dark with no stars where the heart should be, and whatever it eats stays eaten. A seed rides one of us, and where it bursts the floor is gone for good. Carry it to the edge. A fall is a heavy stroke, not a death. Catch the beat.",
    houndmaster: "Old bull of a tree, tall as a tower, black split in his heart that eats and doesn't give back. Seed rides one of us. Where it bursts the floor's gone for good, so walk it out to the edge. A fall's a bruise, not the end. Kept the dogs well back. Heel.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The old tree is down, and the grove is quiet. The room was ours in the end, the holes at the edge and nobody lost in the middle. We kept the ground that mattered. The line came out whole. Will hold.",
    gloomsworn: "The tree is down and the dark in his heart has closed. The holes are still there. What it eats stays gone, even now. The middle was whole and the party stood on it. I know about starved things. The watch is relieved.",
    thresher: "He's down, dear, and the dark in his heart has shut like a door. A great many holes at the edge and none in the middle, which is tidy. His name is off the list. In time, for all of us!",
    bloodreaver: "The big one's down, and the black wound in his heart has shut! I have a fall's worth of scars and none I'd trade. The holes are still there, all round the rim, like a crown. Thank you, my friend. A fine scar.",
    runeguard: "The Starved Grove is felled, and it is entered in the margin with every seed and where it burst. The holes lie round the edge like a ring of inscriptions, and the middle stands. As foreseen, and well kept.",
    gorgemaw: "My host is felled, and the Hunger closed its mouth at last. A lean larder and a mean feast, and he ate his own grove. I was never so well fed by so hungry a cook. Hollow rim, whole middle. Nearly full.",
    oathbroker: "The Grove is concluded: felled, the Hunger closed, the holes entered at the rim and none at the heart. What was taken stays taken, and the account is closed. We kept the floor that mattered. Countersigned.",
    ironpalm: "He spoke only hunger, and we answered by choosing where it fed. The old tree is down, and the dark has closed. The holes ring the edge. The middle is still ours. I breathed.",
    ranger: "Down. Heartwood shut. Holes round the rim, none in the middle, same as planned. Walked off whole. Clean line.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is down and the dark in his heart has closed its eye. Every hole at the edge, none in the middle. He never saw us choose. It never saw me, and neither did the Hunger.",
    pyromancer: "He's down! A whole tree, and I burned a sizeable part of him! The dark in his heart shut, the holes ring the room like a crown, and the middle is mine. My sleeves are gone, the eyebrows are a rumour. Mostly unsinged.",
    thermist: "Down, and the dark in his heart has closed. Cold: holes at the rim, the middle whole. Hot: I could dance on it. I am exactly the temperature of the room again. Back to the middle. The right one.",
    driftblade: "He's down and the dark in his heart shut and I ran the whole middle of the grove just because I could, through where the leaves fell and round the edges where the holes sit, and never landed, and never fell.",
    alembist: "Specimen: felled; the dark in his heartwood closed. Holes: all at the rim, none in the middle. Flasks intact. One leaf pressed in my notebook. Label: the Hunger, spent. Never so pleased.",
    threadweaver: "He's down, and the dark in his heart has been stitched shut. Holes all round the hem, none in the middle, and every one of my stitches walked off it. Come and sit by me, the whole lot of you. Kettle's on.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain for our leading man, and the dark in his heart closed like a stage door. The holes ring the boards, none in the middle. (The Doll took her bow in the one clear spot. Ovation.)",
    cleric: "The old tree is at rest, poor thing, and the dark in his heart has closed. Every hole is at the edge, and every one of my dear ones stood on whole ground at the end. I said a prayer for him. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "He's down, dear, and the dark in him has closed like a wound healing badly. Holes round the edge, none in the middle. I'll plant something in the heartwood when it's cool. Sit. Tea. Plant first.",
    chronomancer: "He fell, and nothing needed undoing. The holes are at the rim, as I'd have planned, and did. The middle is whole, and so are we. I will have been proud. I am.",
    tidecaller: "The tree is down and the dark in his heart is still, slack water at last. Holes at the rim, the middle whole. What the Hunger took, it keeps. I sang over him, low. The sea gives back; the Hunger never did.",
    lanternkeeper: "Four of us walked off whole ground. The dark in his heart closed, and I lit one small lamp where it had been and left it burning. The holes are at the rim, none in the middle. Walk where it's warm.",
    kenseier: "He is down, and the dark in his heart is shut. Every hole at the edge, the middle whole, and the last stroke was his own. I bowed to the old tree. Ash settles.",
    houndmaster: "Old bull's down, black split in his heart shut. Holes round the rim, none in the middle, dogs fine. Good dogs. We had him by supper.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Hunger Seed': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The seed sat over one of the line, never me. They walked it to the rim and stepped out of the ring the instant it stopped following, as the ground split. The rest kept clear of it, and I kept the Oak pinned in the middle. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A seed settled over one of the others, with a ring closing round it. They carried it to the edge and stepped out as the ring set and the ground opened. The rest kept off it. Better the hole at the rim than at the heart. I have lain in worse holes.",
      thresher: "A dark seed hung over one of us, dear, with a ring shrinking round it. Off to the edge it went, and out of the ring the moment it set and the ground opened. Everyone else kept clear. Such a tidy hole, at the rim. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "The seed sat over my friend's head and the ring closed round them! Out to the edge they ran, and out of the ring the moment it stopped following, as the ground split. The rest stayed away. A hole at the rim is a fine scar. A hole in the middle is a stupid one.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a dark seed over one of us, the ring shrinking. The answer: carry it to the edge, step out of the ring as it sets and the ground opens, and the rest stay clear of it. A hole at the rim; none in the middle. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A dark seed over one guest's head, like a cherry on a very bitter cake. We carried it to the rim, the guest stepped out of the ring as the ground split, and the rest kept their plates away. A hole at the edge wastes little. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "The seed is a warrant served upon one of the undersigned. The fine print: where it bursts, the floor is forfeit for good. It is carried to the edge, the bearer steps out of the ring as it sets, and the others stay clear of it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A seed over one of us, a ring closing. They carried it to the edge, and stepped out of the ring when it stopped following and the ground opened. The rest kept away. We chose where the hole would go. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Seed over one of us, ring shrinking. Carried it to the edge, stepped out as it set and the ground split. Rest stayed clear. Hole at the rim, out of every lane. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A dark seed over one of us, with a ring closing, which is no way to treat a guest. They slipped it out to the edge and stepped from the ring as the ground split. The rest of us were nowhere near. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A seed pulsing over a friend's head, ring shrinking like a fuse! They carried it all the way to the edge and stepped out as the ground split, and I stayed well clear and kept casting. A hole at the rim, not a hair of mine in it. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: a seed over one of us, a ring closing, the ground about to go. Carry it to the edge. Step out as it sets. Hot read: the rest of us scattered from it like sparks. Too far? No, far enough. Back to the middle, where the floor is.",
      driftblade: "The seed settled over one of us and the ring started closing, so they ran it out through the whole room to the very edge and stepped out of it as it set and the ground split, while the rest of us kept off the ring and round it.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a dark seed, one carrier, a ring closing round them. Remedy: carry it to the edge, step out of the ring as it sets and the ground opens, everyone else clear. Note: the hole is permanent. Label: at the rim, never the middle.",
      threadweaver: "The seed hung on one of my stitches with a ring closing round it. I watched them draw it out to the very edge, away from the rest of the hem, and step out as the ground split. A neat seam, at the rim. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "A dark seed over one of the company, ring closing: a cue for a long exit. They carried it to the wings, to the very edge, and stepped out of the ring as the boards opened. The rest kept clear. (A trapdoor at the rim, never centre stage.)",
      cleric: "A dark seed over one of my dears, a ring closing round them. They carried it out to the edge and stepped from the ring as the ground opened, and the others kept clear. I mended them before it burst, not after. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "A seed over one of my lot, planted in the wrong soil. They carried it out to the edge and stepped from the ring as the ground opened. A hole at the rim does the least harm. The rest stayed clear. Plant the cure first, and place the wound.",
      chronomancer: "The seed hung over one of us, the ring shrinking toward a beat I could hear. They carried it to the edge and stepped out as the ground split. The rest were clear already. I'll have said the rim, and I did.",
      tidecaller: "A dark seed over one of us, and a ring closing in like a tide coming in. They carried it to the edge, to the shallows, and stepped out as the ground opened. The rest kept off. A hole at the rim does the least harm. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "A dark seed over one of us. I walked beside them to the edge, with a light on them the whole way, and they stepped out of the ring as the ground split. The rest kept away. One carrier, three of us clear. I'll leave one burning.",
      kenseier: "The seed has a beat: the ring closes, then it sets, then the ground splits. They carried it to the edge and stepped out on the set. The rest stayed off the ring. A slow courtesy from the old tree. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Seed over one of us. Walked it out to the rim and stepped clear as the ground split. Dogs kept off the ring, me too. Hole at the edge costs nothing, hole in the middle costs the kennel. Heel.",
    } },
    'Inhale': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He drew breath and everything slid toward him. A hole between us would have swallowed whoever slid across it. I walked the Oak round so none lay in the way, and the line stood where nothing lay between us and him. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He breathed in, and the whole room slid toward the split in his chest. Anyone with a hole in their path went into it. I walked him round till the way was clear, and the rest stood where they could slide clean. I have been pulled underground before.",
      thresher: "He breathes in, dear, and everything leans toward him. If a hole lies between you and his heart, you fall into it on the way. So our shield walked him round until the way was clear, and the rest of us stood where no hole lay between. Do mind the step.",
      bloodreaver: "He drew breath and the whole room slid for his heart! Anyone with a hole in the road went in it. I walked him round till the road was clean, and the others stood where nothing lay between them and him. A good pull, spoiled by good planning.",
      runeguard: "The omen: dust, leaves and light stream toward his heart before he pulls. The answer: I walk him round so no hole lies on the road to him, and the rest stand where the way is clear. Else the drag ends in a hole. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host inhaled, and the whole table slid toward his plate. A hole between a guest and the host is a trapdoor. So I walked him round till the path was clear, and everyone sat where nothing lay in between. Eager, but undisciplined.",
      oathbroker: "He draws breath and all parties are drawn toward him. The fine print: a hole lying between a party and him is a forfeit. I walked him round until the road to him was clear of holes, and the rest took positions where nothing lay between. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed in. We were drawn toward him, and any hole in the way would have taken us. I walked him round until the way was clear, and the others stood where nothing lay between us and him. He breathed out. Breathe.",
      ranger: "He drew breath. Everything slid toward him. Hole in the line, you fall in. Walked him round till the line was clear, rest stood where nothing lay between them and him. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark drew breath and the room slid toward his heart, which is no way to greet anyone. A hole between you and him swallows you on the way. Our shield turned him till every road was clear, and I stood with nothing between us. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He inhaled and the whole room slid toward the split in his chest like moths to a lamp, and a hole in the way was a nasty surprise! The knight walked him round till every road was clear, and I stood where nothing lay between us. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: he breathes in, everything slides toward his heart, and a hole in the way eats whoever crosses it. Walk him round until the way is clear. Hot read: I did not enjoy the slide. Back to the middle. The clean middle.",
      driftblade: "He breathed in and everything slid toward him and anyone with a hole on the way to him fell in, so our shield walked him round till the way was clear and I stood where there was a clean run to his feet, and slid through it, never landing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the drag toward his heart; a hole between you and him is a fall. Remedy: the knight walks him round until no hole lies on the road, and the rest stand where the road is clear. Note: stand before the breath, not during.",
      threadweaver: "He drew breath and every thread went taut toward his heart. A hole in the way is a snag that drops you. The knight walked him round till the road was clear, and I made sure none of my stitches stood behind a hole. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "He inhaled and the company was dragged toward him like a curtain in a draught. A trapdoor between a player and him is fatal. Our shield turned him till the way was clear, and we blocked ourselves clear of every hole. (Exit, pursued by a draught.)",
      cleric: "He drew a great breath and everything slid toward his heart. I told my dears to stand where no hole lay between them and him, and our knight walked him round till the way was clear. A fall is no fate for a friend. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "He drew breath like a wind pulling in before a storm. Anything with a hole in its road goes in. The knight walked him round till the road was clear and my lot stood where nothing lay between them and him. Plant first, and clear the field.",
      chronomancer: "The inhale comes a beat after the light starts streaming. We stood where no hole lay between us and him, and our knight walked him round beforehand. I will have warned them. I did.",
      tidecaller: "He drew breath like a tide running out, and everything went with it. A hole in the way is a rip. The knight walked him round till the way was clear, and the rest of us stood with clean water between us and him. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "Dust and leaves and light streamed toward his heart before the pull. I lit the clear road ahead as the knight walked him round, so no hole lay between any of us and him. Four of us, one clean road each. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "His breath in has a long count, and the light streams toward him before it. I walked him round on that count until no hole lay on the way, and the others stood where the road was clear. A blade cannot turn it. Breathe, then strike.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad breathed in and the whole lot of us slid toward him. Hole in the way, you drop in it. Walked him round till the road was clear, dogs and all. Stood where nothing lay between us and him. Heel.",
    } },
    'Fourfold Bloom': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Four petals opened round him, two wide, two narrow, all red. I stood in a gap and held, and when they snapped I stepped round the way they leaned, keeping to the gap, before they snapped again. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Four petals opened, two broad, two thin, and snapped shut. They turned a little the way they leaned and snapped again. I stood in a gap, walked round with the turn, and stayed in the gap. A gap is generous compared to a coffin.",
      thresher: "Four great petals, dear, two broad and two thin, and every one of them red. They snap shut, then lean and turn a little and snap again. Stand in the gap between, and step round the way they lean. A tidy waltz. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "Four petals, two fat, two thin, all red, all hungry! They snapped, turned a little the way they leaned, and snapped again. I stood in a gap and went round with the turn. The gaps were a gift. Almost shameful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: four petals, two broad and two narrow, and they lean the way they will turn. The answer: stand in a gap, and after the first snap, turn with them, keeping the gap, before the second. Thorn, frost, spark, blight, in that order. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Four petals like four courses, thorn, frost, spark and blight, two broad and two thin, all of them red. They snap, turn a little the way they lean, and snap again. I sat in the gap and turned with the table. Four courses, none for me.",
      oathbroker: "Four petals, two broad and two narrow, all payable in red. They close, turn a little in the direction they lean, and close again. The clause: stand in a gap and move with the turn, keeping to the gap, before the second closing. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Four petals opened, two broad, two narrow. They snapped, leaned, turned a little, and snapped again. I stood in the gap between them, and turned with them. One word, then the next. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Four petals, two wide, two narrow, all red. They snap, lean, turn a little, snap again. Stood in a gap. Went round with the turn, same gap. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Four petals open round him, which is rude, and snap shut, then lean and turn a little and snap again, which is ruder. I stood in a gap and turned with them, keeping to it. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Four huge petals, thorn and frost and spark and blight, all glowing the colour of trouble! They snapped, leaned, turned a little and snapped again. I stood in a gap and turned with them, and resented every step. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: four petals, two wide, two narrow, a gap between each. They snap, lean, turn a little, snap. Stand in a gap. Hot read: turn with them, quick, before the second. Too slow and it catches you. Back to the gap.",
      driftblade: "Four petals opened round him, two wide, two thin, and snapped, and leaned and turned a little and snapped again, so I stood in a gap and went round with them, through the turn and out the far side, never landing, the gap keeping step.",
      alembist: "Ailment: four petals, two wide, two narrow, all red; they snap, lean, turn a little, snap again. Remedy: stand in a gap between them, and after the first snap turn with them, keeping to the gap. Label: the petals show the turn.",
      threadweaver: "Four petals opened round him, two broad and two thin, and snapped, then leaned and turned a little and snapped again. I stood in a gap, and turned with them, keeping the seam straight. A sloppy stitch is a bad place to stand. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "Four petals, two broad, two thin, all red: the stage directions. They snap shut, lean, turn a little, snap again. Stand in a gap, and turn with the set. (Exit through the gap, always. Never through a petal.)",
      cleric: "Four petals opened round him, two broad and two thin, all red. They snapped, leaned, turned a little and snapped again. I stood in a gap and turned with them, and called my dears to do likewise. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Four petals opened, two broad and two thin, like a flower in a bad dream. They close, lean the way they will turn, and close again. I stood in a gap and turned with the bloom. Even a bad flower follows the sun.",
      chronomancer: "Four petals, and the second snap comes a beat after the first, a little to the side. They lean the way they will turn, so I stood in a gap and turned with them. I'll have been there already. I was.",
      tidecaller: "Four petals opened like a wave rearing, two broad, two thin. They snapped, leaned, turned a little and snapped again. I stood in the gap between them and turned with them, like a boat turning with the swell. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "Four petals opened round him, two broad and two thin, all red. I stood in a gap, and when they snapped I went round with the turn, still in the gap, and was there before they closed again. Four gaps, four of us. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "Four petals: two broad, two thin, a stroke, a small turn, and a second stroke. They lean the way they will turn. I stood in a gap and turned with them, on the beat. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Four petals round the big lad, two broad, two thin, all red. They snap, lean, turn a bit, snap again. Stood in a gap and went round with them, dogs on my heels. Like walking a dog round a post. Heel.",
    } },
    'Root Lash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The roots came for me alone, a heavy lash that holds whoever it touches. I walked him off the holes before it fell, since I couldn't walk after. The rest kept off the ring that follows me. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The lash was meant for me, and it held whoever stood near. A wound is only a wound. I walked him off the holes first, because I'd be standing still after. The rest kept out of my ring.",
      thresher: "Roots whip up around our shield, dear, a heavy blow that holds anyone it touches. The ring follows him, so keep out of it, and he moves the old fellow off the holes before, not after. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "The roots whipped up at me and held me like a lover! A good one. I walked him off the holes first, since I couldn't walk after, and the others stayed out of the ring around me. Bleed first, then drink.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the roots stir in the ground at the shield's feet. The answer: I lead him off the holes first, for the lash holds me still; the rest stay out of my ring, and the healer's ward goes up before. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Roots, whipped into my face, a heavy, woody dish that held me in place while I ate. I walked the host off the holes before the course arrived. The others kept out of the ring around me. Tough, and fibrous.",
      oathbroker: "The lash is a writ upon whoever stands before him: heavy, and binding whoever is near. I move him off the holes before it falls, since I cannot after. The others keep out of the ring about me. Accepted in full. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The roots rose to answer me, a heavy word that holds. Before it fell, I led him off the holes. The others stayed out of the ring around me. I took it, and could not move for a moment. Then I answered. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Roots whip up at the shield. Heavy, and it holds whoever's near. He walked the beast off the holes first. Rest stayed out of the ring around him. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark lashed at our shield with roots that hold, which is pawing. The ring follows him, so I kept out of it, and he'd walked the mark off the holes first. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The roots whipped up at the knight, heavy as a house, and held anything near him! I stayed out of the ring and kept casting; he'd walked the old brute off the holes first. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: roots at the shield's feet, a heavy blow, a ring that follows him, and anyone in it is held. Keep out. Hot read: walk him off the holes before it lands, not after, and the others all out of the ring. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The roots came up at our shield, heavy, and held whoever was in the ring following him, so I stayed out of it and circled while he walked the old tree off the holes before it landed, because nobody walks anywhere once held.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a heavy lash upon the shield, and roots that hold whoever is near. Remedy: walk him off the holes first, the rest out of the ring, the mending laid before. Note: held people cannot step away from anything.",
      threadweaver: "The roots whipped at our knight, a heavy blow that holds whoever is near. The rest of my stitches stayed out of the ring about him, and he walked the old tree off the holes first. A tidy seam. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "A heavy lash for our shield: roots out of the boards, holding anyone near. The company stayed out of the ring about him, and he walked the star off the trapdoors before the cue. (Exit, held, stage left. Not us.)",
      cleric: "The roots lashed our knight, a heavy blow that holds whoever is close. I laid the light on him before it fell, and the rest kept out of the ring about him. He walked the old tree off the holes first. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "Roots whipped up at the knight like a bad frost, holding anyone near. I'd planted on him before. My lot kept out of the ring about him, and he'd walked the old oak off the holes. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The lash lands a beat after the ring locks, and holds whoever is in it. The knight walked him off the holes before, I mended him early, the rest stayed out. I'll have known that. I did.",
      tidecaller: "A heavy lash for our knight, and roots that hold whoever is close, like kelp round an ankle. I let the water rise on him first. The rest stayed out of the ring about him, and he walked the old tree off the holes. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "The roots whipped at our knight, a heavy blow that holds whoever is near. I hung a light on him before it fell and stayed out of the ring. He had walked the old tree off the holes first. One lamp, one blow.",
      kenseier: "The lash is aimed at me, and has a count. I met it as it landed, and the roots held whoever was near, so the rest stayed out of the ring. I walked him off the holes first. An honest blow. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Roots up at the shield-man, and anyone near got held. Dogs kept out of the ring, me too. He walked the old bull off the holes first, since he'd not walk after. Sit. Good dogs.",
    } },
    'Sap Tide': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Amber sap welled up out of the ground and reached every one of us. Nothing to block, so I planted my feet and let the line close up near the healer. Each time it came heavier than the last. Held, barely.",
      gloomsworn: "The ground wept amber, and it reached all of us. There was nothing to hide behind, and each wave was heavier than the one before. We stood close to the healer and took it. I have drowned in worse than sap.",
      thresher: "Amber sap rises from the ground, dear, and reaches every one of us, and each wave a little heavier than the last. Stay close to the healer and get topped up before, not after. Do mind the step.",
      bloodreaver: "Sap came up from the ground and hit every one of us! Nowhere to hide, and each time it hit harder than the time before. I like him better for it. Stay near the healer, get filled up first, and take it. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: amber wells up from the roots. The blast reaches every one of us, and each is heavier than the last: his hunger grows. The answer: stand close to the healer, fully mended before it lands. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Sap, amber and sweet and poured over the entire room at once. Each course came heavier than the last. We gathered around the healer and swallowed it together. He serves it sweet, then sweeter. Cloying, but filling.",
      oathbroker: "The Tide is levied upon all parties at once, amber sap rising from the roots, and each instalment is larger than the last. The healer's cover must be laid before, and the undersigned gathered near. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The ground wept amber. It spoke to all of us at once, and each time more loudly. We gathered close to the healer and listened together, mended beforehand. Then we answered. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Amber sap up out of the ground. Hits everyone. Heavier each time. No lane to take. Closed up on the healer, topped up before. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Sap rose through the roots and reached every one of us, and each time it came heavier, which is greedy. No shadow to hide in. I stood close to the healer, mended beforehand. It never saw me, but sap isn't picky.",
      pyromancer: "Amber sap welled out of the ground and hit every one of us, and every wave was bigger than the last! I stood close to the healer, mended before, and kept casting through it. Only my sleeve stuck to the floor.",
      thermist: "Cold read: sap wells up, amber, from the roots, and reaches every one of us, heavier every time. Close to the healer, mended before. Hot read: the later ones made me run hot with fright. Steadied. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The ground wept amber and it reached everybody and there was nowhere to run, so I ran to the healer instead, through the others, and stood mended before it came, and every wave was heavier than the last, so I ran in sooner each time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: amber sap through the whole room, heavier each wave. Remedy: gather close to the healer, mending brewed beforehand, not after. Note: the dose grows, so the brew must too. Label: sticky.",
      threadweaver: "Sap welled up and reached every thread at once, and each wave pulled harder than the last. I drew my stitches in close, mended them before it landed, and let it run through us together. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "The set wept amber, a blast that reached the whole house, and each night's performance heavier than the last. The company gathered near the healer, mended in the wings beforehand. (Standing ovation, for the healer.)",
      cleric: "Amber sap came up from the ground and reached every one of us, heavier each time. I gathered my dears close first, so one prayer could reach them all, and mended them before, not after. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The sap rises, and rises more each season. It reached every one of us. My lot gathered close and I mended them before, since a late frost is no use to the crop. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The sap came up the same way every time, a little heavier each time, and every one of us was hit. We gathered close a beat before, and I mended early. The last was heavier than the first, which I knew.",
      tidecaller: "Amber sap, rising like a spring tide, a little higher each time and reaching every one of us. I let my water rise early so it was high when the sap broke, and we stood close together. The sea gives back.",
      lanternkeeper: "Sap welled up out of the ground and reached every one of us, a little heavier each time. I lit the healing early, and we stood close, one glow for all of us, topped up before the wicks burned low. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "The sap has no beat I can turn. It came up amber across the room, heavier each time, and a blade cannot meet it. We stood in close to the healer, mended before, and outlasted it. Ash settles.",
      houndmaster: "Sap came up out of the ground and hit the lot of us, dogs too. Heavier every time. Nowhere to run, so we stood in tight by the healer, mended first. Easy, lads.",
    } },
    'Saplings': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Little trees sprouted and set off for the nearest hole. If one reaches it, the hole grows. So the line broke them before they got there, the nearest first, and I kept the Oak still. A hole that grows eats our ground. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Saplings sprouted and started walking for the holes. One that arrives makes the hole wider. We cut them down on the way, the nearest first. Felling saplings is easier than burying them. I have done both.",
      thresher: "Little saplings pop up, dear, and toddle off to the nearest hole, and the hole gets wider for each one that arrives. Cut them down on the road, the closest to a hole first. Such small appointments. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "Little trees! They marched for the nearest hole, and each one that got there made it wider. I smashed the ones nearest the holes first and then the rest. They barely hurt. Shameful. A fine afternoon's work.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the saplings sprout at the edge and walk to the nearest hole. The answer: break them on the road, the nearest to a hole first, for each that arrives widens it. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Saplings, served in a ring, each walking to the nearest hole like starters carried to a bad table. One that arrives makes the hole bigger. We ate them on the way, nearest first. Bland, but a pleasant crunch.",
      oathbroker: "The saplings are agents of the counterparty. Each walks to the nearest hole and, on arriving, enlarges it. We broke them on the road, the nearest to a hole first, before the breach was widened. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Little trees rose and walked to the holes. Each one that arrived made its hole wider. We broke them on the way, the nearest first, each a short word. The holes stayed small. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Saplings sprout and walk for the nearest hole. One arrives, hole widens. Shot them down on the road, nearest to a hole first. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Little trees sprouted and went toddling for the holes, and each one that arrives makes its hole wider. I cut them down from behind, the nearest first. They never saw me, which is a talent in a sapling.",
      pyromancer: "Little trees! I could have wept with joy. They marched for the nearest hole, and each one to arrive made it wider, so I burned the nearest to a hole first and then the rest. A forest, now ash. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: saplings sprout and walk to the nearest hole, and each that arrives widens it. Break them on the road, the nearest first. Hot read: I burned a few for sport. Too far, perhaps. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Little trees sprang up and ran for the nearest hole and each one that arrived made it wider, so I went through the lot, nearest first, and round the next and back through, and not a sapling reached the edge.",
      alembist: "Ailment: saplings, walking to the nearest hole; each that arrives widens it. Remedy: break them on the road, nearest to a hole first. Note: they are small and soft. Label: weed early.",
      threadweaver: "Saplings sprouted and walked for the nearest hole, and each one that arrived pulled the hole wider like a loose thread. We snipped them on the road, the nearest first. A sloppy hem, otherwise. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "Little understudies came on, walking to the nearest trapdoor, and each that arrived made it wider. The company cut them down on their way, the nearest first. (Exit, the understudies. No bows.)",
      cleric: "Little saplings sprouted and set off for the holes, poor things, and each one that arrived made its hole wider. We broke them on the road, the nearest to a hole first. I do not enjoy it. Thank the light for quick hands.",
      grovekeeper: "Saplings, sprouted out of season and heading for the holes. I was sorry to see them cut, but each one that reached a hole widened it. The nearest to a hole went first. Even weeds are seedlings once. Plant first, and thin early.",
      chronomancer: "The saplings sprout, and a few beats later they reach the nearest hole and widen it. We broke them on the road, the nearest first. I'll have been quicker, but I wasn't.",
      tidecaller: "Small things, many of them, walking for the nearest hole like water finding a drain, and each one that arrives widens it. We broke them on the way, the nearest first. The shore is worn down this way too. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "Saplings sprouted round the edge and walked for the nearest hole, and each one that arrived widened it. I lit the ones nearest the holes so we saw them first, and we broke them on the road. Four hands, many saplings. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "Little trees, each with a short walk to its hole. Each one that arrives widens it. I stepped through the nearest and cut them down, nearest to a hole first. Small work. Breathe, then strike.",
      houndmaster: "Little trees walked off to the nearest hole, and each one that got there made it wider. Put the dogs on them, nearest first, and I broke the rest. Rust would've found every last one, silly lad. Good dogs.",
    } },
    'The Swallowing': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The grove tore loose and fell. A blast reached the line first, so we stood close to the healer for it. Then we rode the ground down, no harm in the ride, and the holes came down with us. Sap and the lash followed the landing. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The grove tore loose and fell, with us on it. A blast reached all of us first. After that nothing to do but ride the pieces down. The holes came with us, so I knew where the ground was. I have fallen further.",
      thresher: "Down goes the whole grove, dear, with us on it! A blast reaches everyone first, so stand close to the healer. Then ride the pieces down, nothing to dodge, and mind the holes, for they come down with us. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole grove tore loose and fell, and we rode it down! A blast hit all of us first, and then nothing, just falling. A lovely drop. The holes fell with us, so I knew where not to stand. Sap and the lash came right after. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a blast across the whole grove, and then the ground tears loose and falls. The answer: gather near the healer for the blast, then ride the pieces down; nothing to dodge. The holes come with us, and a sap and a lash follow the landing. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host swallowed the whole grove, and us with it. A blast first, shared by all, then a long ride down on the pieces, nothing to chew on. The holes came with us, like bones in the stew. Sap and lash for pudding.",
      oathbroker: "The grove is surrendered: a blast upon all parties, then the ground falls away and we ride it down. No penalty attaches to the ride. The holes are carried with us. The sap and the lash fall due directly after the landing. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke once to everyone, and the grove fell. We took the blast together, then rode the pieces down without a blow. The holes came with us. When we landed, two more words followed, sap and lash. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Blast on everyone. Then the grove tore loose and fell. Rode the pieces down. Nothing to dodge. Holes came with us. Sap and lash right after the landing. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark swallowed the grove, and us with it, which seems a little grabby. A blast reaches everyone first, so I stood close to the healer, then rode the pieces down in comfort. The holes came with us. It never saw me fall.",
      pyromancer: "The whole grove fell! A blast hit everyone first, and then nothing to dodge, just a long glorious ride on the pieces. The holes came with us, glowing, rude. Sap and the lash right after we landed. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: a blast on everyone, then the whole grove falls and we ride it down. Nothing to dodge. Hot read: it was glorious. The holes fell with us, so I knew where not to land. Sap and the lash, just after. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "A blast hit everyone first and then the grove tore loose and fell and I rode a piece of it down like a gust, hopping through the others, and the holes fell with us so I knew where not to land, and the sap and the lash came right after.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a blast through the whole grove; then it falls. Remedy: gather close to the healer for the blast, then ride the pieces down; nothing to dodge. Note: the holes fall with us, and sap and the lash come right after. Label: brace twice.",
      threadweaver: "A blast reached every thread at once, and then the grove tore loose and fell, with us on it. I kept my stitches close for the blast and rode the pieces down. The holes came along. A sap and a lash right after. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "Act break! A blast across the house, and the whole stage falls away beneath us. The company gathered near the healer for the blast, then rode the pieces down. (The trapdoors fell with us. Sap, then lash, at the landing.)",
      cleric: "A blast reached us all, and then the grove tore loose and fell with us on it. I gathered my dears for the blast, and we rode the pieces down. The holes came with us. Sap and lash right after the landing. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The whole grove tore loose and fell with us in it, like a field turned over by a flood. A blast first, shared by all, then a long fall on the pieces. The holes came with us. Sap and a lash after the landing. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "A blast first, and a beat after it the grove falls. We stood close to the healer for the blast and rode the pieces down, with the holes. Sap and the lash a beat after landing. I'll have been ready.",
      tidecaller: "The blast broke over every one of us, and then the whole grove tore loose and fell, like a shelf of ice into the sea. We rode the pieces down. The holes came with us. Sap and lash on the landing. The sea gives back.",
      lanternkeeper: "A blast reached us all, and then the grove fell. We stood close for the blast and I lit our way down. The holes came with us, so I lit the edge of each. Sap and the lash were waiting at the landing. Walk where it's warm.",
      kenseier: "A blast with no beat a blade could turn, and then the grove fell with us on it. We rode the pieces down, and the holes came with us. Sap and the lash a few beats after the landing. A strange teacher. Ash settles.",
      houndmaster: "Whole grove tore loose and fell with us on it, dogs too. A blast first, then a long ride down on the pieces, nothing to do but hold on. Holes fell with us. Sap and the lash right after we landed. Easy, lads.",
    } },
    'The Grove Remade': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He rose out of the fall wearing four limbs, grown from the monsters we've beaten: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Break the one you can't stand, in whatever order, and its blow is gone. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He rose in a new shape, wearing four limbs grown from things we had killed. A cleaver, roots, a storm, a lantern. Each can be broken on its own, and a broken limb takes its blow with it. Old faces, mostly. I have been among the dead before.",
      thresher: "He rises again, dear, wearing four limbs grown from old guests of ours: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken one takes its trick with it. Choose the order. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "He rose wearing four limbs grown from monsters I've bled! The butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern! Each has its own bar, and a broken one takes its blow with it. Break them in whatever order you like. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: four limbs, grown from the beaten. The Butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the Hollow King's lantern. The answer: each has its own bar, and a limb broken takes its move away. The order is ours to choose. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host rose again wearing four dishes I had already eaten: Grubnak's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the Hollow King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a limb broken is a course struck from the menu. The order is ours. Leftovers.",
      oathbroker: "The counterparty emerged with four limbs, grown from parties we have already settled: the Butcher, the Broodmother, the Storm, the Hollow King. Each limb has its own bar. A limb broken forfeits its blow. The order of settlement is ours. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He rose, wearing four old voices: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each limb has its own bar. Break one, and its voice is gone. The order is ours to choose. Breathe.",
      ranger: "He rose wearing four limbs from game I'd already taken. Butcher's cleaver. Broodmother's roots. Storm's knot. King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Break one, its blow's gone. Pick the order. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark returned wearing four limbs, stolen from old marks of ours: the butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken limb takes its blow with it. We chose the order. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He rose in a bigger shape wearing four limbs from monsters I've burned! The butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken one takes its blow with it. I chose the order. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: four limbs, grown from the monsters we beat: cleaver, roots, storm, lantern. Each has its own bar. A limb broken loses its blow. Hot read: break them in whatever order we like! Too eager? Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "He rose wearing four limbs, the butcher's cleaver and the spider's roots and the Storm's knot and the King's lantern, each with its own bar, and I went through them in whatever order suited, and every one broken took its blow with it.",
      alembist: "Specimen: second form; four limbs, grown from the butcher, the Broodmother, the Storm and the King. Ailment: each blow in turn. Remedy: each limb has its own bar; break one and its blow is gone. Note: the order is ours. Label: pick the worst first.",
      threadweaver: "He rose wearing four limbs, sewn on from monsters we'd beaten: the butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a limb snipped off takes its trick with it. The order is ours. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "Second act! Our leading man returns wearing four costumes from old productions: the butcher's cleaver, the spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each limb has its own bar, and a broken one takes its scene with it. (We chose the order.)",
      cleric: "He rose again wearing four limbs, grown from the poor creatures we put to rest. The butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken limb takes its blow away. The order is ours. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "He rose grafted, dear: four limbs from monsters we'd put in the ground. The butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Prune one and its blow is gone. We chose the order. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The limbs were all familiar, which was the joke: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern, each with its own bar. A broken limb loses its blow, and I will have chosen the order. I did.",
      tidecaller: "He rose out of the deep wearing four limbs from old wrecks: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar, and a broken limb takes its blow with it. The order is ours. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "He rose wearing four limbs, and one of them is the King's own lantern, a pale one. Cleaver, roots, storm, lantern: each from a monster we'd put out. Each has its own bar. A broken limb takes its blow away. Four limbs, four turns. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "Four limbs, grown from teachers we have already met: the butcher's cleaver, the Broodmother's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. A limb broken takes its stroke with it. The order is ours. Breathe, then strike.",
      houndmaster: "He rose with four limbs sewn on, all from game we'd taken: butcher's cleaver, spider's roots, the Storm's knot, the King's lantern. Each has its own bar. Break one, its blow's gone. Any order suits. Dogs knew them all. Good dogs.",
    } },
    'Cleaver Bough': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A blue ring settled on one of the line, and it wants us all in it, since it's too heavy for one. We stood together. Then a chop came down the line from the bough through the ring, so we stepped off it the moment the blow landed. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A blue ring on one of us. The blow is too heavy for one, so we stood in it together. Then the cleaver came down the line from the bough through where we stood, so we stepped aside. I have been cut worse. Not by a tree.",
      thresher: "A blue ring on one of us, dear, and everyone is meant to stand in it, for the blow is too heavy to carry alone. Then the cleaver chops down the line from the bough through where we stood. Step off it! Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "The butcher's old cleaver on a branch! A blue ring on one of us, and we all piled in to take it together, a heavy one! Then a chop followed down the line from the bough through us, and we got off it. I miss the butcher's cooking.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a blue ring on one of us, painted before the swing. The answer: gather in it, all of us, for the blow is too heavy for one. Then step off the line the chop paints from the bough through the ring. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Grubnak's cleaver, grown on a branch. A blue ring over one guest, and we all sat in it to share the portion, too heavy for one. Then the cleaver fell along the line from the bough through where we sat. We stepped off. Clumsy carving. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "A blue ring is entered against one of us, and the clause is that all of us stand in it: it is too heavy for one. Thereafter the cleaver falls along the line from the bough through the ring. The undersigned vacate the line. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A blue ring on one of us. A heavy word, too heavy to say alone, so we stood in it together. Then the cleaver answered, along the line from the bough through the ring. We stepped aside, and it fell on empty ground. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Blue ring on one of us. Too heavy alone, so all of us in it. Then a chop down the line from the bough through the ring. Off the line. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A blue ring over one of us, and we all crowded in, since the blow is too heavy for one. The chop follows down the line from the bough through the ring, which is predictable of him. I was off the line. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A blue ring over a friend's head and a cleaver the size of a barn! We all piled into the ring, because it's too heavy for one, and then ran off the line the chop paints from the bough through it. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: a blue ring on one of us, too heavy for one, so everyone in it. A chop follows along the line from the bough through the ring. Step off. Hot read: we nearly stood in the line. Too slow. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "A blue ring on one of us and everyone ran into it, because the blow is too heavy for one, and then out again sideways, off the line the chop paints from the bough through the ring, and the cleaver came down on bare floor.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a blue ring on one of us; too heavy for one. Remedy: all of us in it, then off the line the chop paints from the bough through the ring. Note: do not linger in the dose. Label: share, then scatter.",
      threadweaver: "A blue ring over one of my stitches, and the blow was too heavy for one, so I drew the whole hem into it. Then we all stepped off the line the chop painted from the bough through the ring. A neat seam. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "A blue mark on one of the company, and the whole cast gathered on it, too heavy for a solo. Then the cleaver chopped down the line from the bough through the mark. (Exit, all, stage left. Not through the line.)",
      cleric: "A blue ring settled over one of my dears, and the blow was too heavy for one, so we gathered in it together. I mended them before it fell. Then the cleaver came down the line through the ring, and we were already off it. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "A blue ring on one of my lot. Too heavy for one root to bear, so we all stood in it. I planted the cure first. Then the chop came down the line from the bough through the ring, and we'd stepped aside. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The ring comes first, and the chop a beat after it, down the line from the bough through where we stood. We gathered in the ring, then stepped off the line. I will have moved first. I did.",
      tidecaller: "A blue ring on one of us, a swell too heavy for one. We stood in it together and I let the water rise on them beforehand. Then the cleaver came down the line from the bough through the ring, and we'd ebbed aside. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "A blue ring over one of us, too heavy for one. I hung a light in the ring's middle and we all stood in it. Then the cleaver chopped down the line from the bough through the ring, and we were already off it. One ring, all of us. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "A blue ring on one of us, and a heavy stroke: the cleaver. We shared it in the ring. A second stroke follows along the line from the bough through the ring, with a count I could hear. We stepped aside. Honest. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Big blue ring on one of us. Too heavy for one, so in we went, dogs too. Then the chop came down the line from the bough through the ring. Stepped off it. Like a butcher's block. Heel.",
    } },
    'Brood Roots': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Five strands of root were painted out from the limb, strung with grey silk. We stood in the gaps between them, farther out where the gaps are wide. A strand holds whoever it catches. Nobody was caught. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Five root strands, strung with grey silk, painted out from the limb. Whoever is caught is held in place. I stood in a gap between them, farther out where the gaps are wide. Being held is something I have practice at.",
      thresher: "Five strands of root and grey silk, dear, reaching out from the limb, and whoever they catch is held still. The gaps between are safe, and wider further out. Do mind the step. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "Five root strands strung with silk, reaching from the limb! Whoever they grab is stuck to the floor, which is no fun at all. We stood in the gaps, further out where they're wide. Barely a scratch, if you're quick. Shameful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: five strands painted out from the limb, silk-strung. The answer: the gaps between them are safe, and widen with distance from the limb; whoever is caught is held. We stood in the gaps. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Five strands of root and silk, like a spider's tablecloth. Whoever they touch is held to the spot. I sat in the gaps, further out where they are broad. Chewy, and sticky, and not for me.",
      oathbroker: "Five strands are entered, silk-strung, from the limb outward. Any party caught is bound in place. The gaps between them are free of liability, and widen with distance from the limb. The undersigned occupied them. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Five strands reached from the limb, and the Broodmother's silk was in them. Whoever they touch is held. We stood in the gaps, farther out, where there is room. I did not wish to be still. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Five strands out from the limb, silk and root. Whoever's caught is held. Gaps between are clear, wider the further from the limb. Stood in one. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Five strands of root and silk painted from the limb, which is the Broodmother's trick, and she did it better. Whoever is caught is held. I took a gap, farther out, where there's room to stand. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Five strands of root and silk shooting from the limb, and whoever they catch is stuck fast! I do not like being stuck. We took the gaps between them, further out where they are wide, and I kept casting from there. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: five strands from the limb, root and silk. Caught means held. The gaps between are clear. Hot read: stand far out where the gaps widen, and don't dither. Too close and there's no room. Back to the gap.",
      driftblade: "Five strands shot out from the limb, root and grey silk, and whoever they catch is held, so I went out where the gaps are wide and stood in one, and the strands fell on either side of me, never touching, and I never landed.",
      alembist: "Ailment: five strands of root and silk, from the limb; whoever is caught is held. Remedy: stand in a gap between them, farther from the limb where the gaps widen. Note: held people cannot dodge anything else. Label: sticky.",
      threadweaver: "Five strands of root and silk spun out from the limb, the Broodmother's work, and I'd call it clumsy. Whoever they catch is held. My stitches stood in the gaps, further out where there's room. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "Five strands of root and silk, a most irregular set of strings, and anyone caught is held on the spot. The company stood in the gaps, upstage, where the strands are far apart. (Strings are for the Doll.)",
      cleric: "Five strands of root and silk reached out from the limb, and any of my dears caught in them is held still. We stood in the gaps between, farther out where there's room. I called each to a gap by name. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Five strands of root and silk, from the Broodmother, thrown out from the limb like runners. Whoever is caught is held. My lot stood in the gaps between them, further out where they are wide. Even runners leave room. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Five strands, painted first, and they hold whoever they catch. The gaps between are safe, and wide farther from the limb. We stood in them before the strands fell, not after. I'll have done it earlier. I did.",
      tidecaller: "Five strands of root and silk, like nets cast out from the limb. Whoever they catch is held. We stood in the gaps between, farther out where the water is wide. There was no hurry once we had seen the nets. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "Five strands of root and silk reached out from the limb, and whoever they catch is held. I lit the gaps between them, and we stood there, farther out where there's room. Four of us, five gaps. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "Five strands, painted, from the limb: the Broodmother's work, slow and sticky. Whoever they catch is held. We stood in the gaps, farther out where they are wide. I did not draw the blade. A net is answered by where one stands. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Five strands of root and silk off the limb. Rust would've been tangled in a trice. Whoever's caught is held. Stood us all in the gaps, farther out where they're wide. Sit, Rust. Good dogs.",
    } },
    'Storm Knot': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Three rings were painted where three of us stood, with a rod at the middle of each. I walked out of mine and kept out of the others'. The knot is the Storm's, and it has no patience for people who stand still. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Three rods came down, each in a painted ring where one of us was standing. I stepped out of mine and stayed out of the other two. The Storm was a fine fighter. I do not recall the lightning being friendly.",
      thresher: "Three great rings painted on the floor, dear, each wherever someone happened to be standing, with a rod in the middle. Step out of yours, and keep out of the other two. Such a tidy storm. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "Three rods, three rings, each painted where somebody stood! The Storm's lightning, grown on a branch. I walked out of mine and stayed out of the others'. A beautiful bolt, and I wanted one. A fine scar, if I'd stayed.",
      runeguard: "The omen: three rings painted on the floor, a rod at each centre, each where one of us stood. The answer: leave the ring you stand in, and keep clear of the other two. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The Storm's old trick, three rods in three rings, each painted where a guest had been seated. I got up from mine and kept away from the other two. Charred, and too salty.",
      oathbroker: "Three rods are entered, each in its ring, and each ring named where a party stood. The clause: vacate the ring you stand in, and stay out of the other two. The Storm's terms, now borrowed by the Grove. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Three rings were painted, each where one of us stood, and a rod waited in the middle of each. We stepped out of ours and kept out of the others'. The Storm spoke three times, and found nobody home. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Three rings painted, where three of us were standing. Rod at each centre. Walked out of mine, stayed out of the others. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Three rings painted where three of us stood, each with a rod in the middle, which is rude, since I was standing there. I stepped out of mine and kept out of the others'. It never saw me move.",
      pyromancer: "Three rods of lightning, each in a ring painted where somebody stood, and it was glorious! The Storm's own trick, grown on a branch. I stepped out of mine, and kept out of the others', and kept casting. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: three rings painted where three of us stood, a rod in the middle of each. Step out of yours. Stay out of the others'. Hot read: lightning is my kind of weather, but not on me. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Three rings painted on the floor wherever somebody had been standing, and a rod in the middle of each, so I went out of mine and through the gap between the other two and round, never landing, and the lightning struck only floor.",
      alembist: "Ailment: three rods of lightning, each in a painted ring where someone was standing. Remedy: leave your ring at once, and stay out of the other two. Note: the rings are drawn first, so the cure is only to walk. Label: keep the glass away.",
      threadweaver: "Three rings painted wherever someone had been standing, a rod at each centre. Each of my stitches stepped out of their own and kept clear of the other two. A rod is a poor needle. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "Three rings painted wherever the company stood, a rod at each centre: the Storm's old cue. Each of us stepped out of our own and stayed out of the others'. (The lightning struck an empty stage. Reviews: shocked.)",
      cleric: "Three rods came down, each in a ring painted where one of my dears stood. Every one of them stepped out of their own ring and kept out of the other two. The Storm is not a thing to stand under. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The Storm's knot, three rods, and a ring painted wherever someone stood. I moved out of mine, and kept out of the others'. Lightning in a tree is the oldest story. Plant first, and step out of the rings.",
      chronomancer: "The rings are painted first, and the lightning follows a beat behind them. Three of us were under rings. Everybody stepped out of their own, and kept out of the others'. I'll have moved first. Eventually.",
      tidecaller: "Three rings painted where three of us stood, a rod at the heart of each, a squall with edges. I stepped out of mine and kept out of the others'. The Storm used to be weather. Now it is a limb. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three rods came down in three painted rings, each where one of us was standing. I moved my light out of mine and stood outside it, and kept clear of the other two. Three rods, four of us. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "Three rings, a rod in each, painted where three of us stood. Three strokes with a long count between. I stepped out of mine and kept out of the other two. The Storm taught me this once. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Three rings painted where three of us stood, with a rod in each. Walked out of mine, kept out of the other two, dogs at heel. Never liked lightning in a field. Heel.",
    } },
    'Barrow Lantern': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A pale light settled on one of us and burst on whoever stood near. Then it passed to the farthest of us, and burst again. So the marked one stood apart from the line, and the line kept off. When it jumped, the new one walked clear. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A pale lantern light marks one of us, and bursts around them. Then it passes to whoever stands farthest away. We kept apart from the marked one and walked clear when it passed. The lantern is the King's. I knew him by his light.",
      thresher: "A pale light on one of us, dear, and it bursts around them, then passes to whoever stands farthest away. Keep away from the marked one, and when it comes to you, go off alone. A grave-lantern and good manners. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "A pale light settled on one of us and burst around them! Then it jumped to whoever was farthest away, and burst again. The marked one stayed clear of the others, and the rest kept off. The King's own lantern, and I can't stop smiling. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a pale lantern mark settles on one of us. It bursts around them, then passes to the farthest from them, on a beat. The answer: the marked one stands apart; the rest keep clear; the next to be marked walks away. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The Hollow King's lantern, grown on a branch: a pale glow on one guest, bursting around them, then passed on to the guest sitting farthest away. The marked one sat apart; the rest kept their plates clear. A cold, funereal dish.",
      oathbroker: "The Barrow's lantern is a writ passed hand to hand. It marks one party, discharges around them, and transfers to the party farthest away, on a beat. The marked party stands apart; the rest keep clear; the next vacates at once. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A pale light settled on one of us, and spoke, and passed to the farthest of us, and spoke again. Whoever was marked stood apart, and the rest kept off. When it came to them, they walked away. A call, then a call. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Pale light on one of us. Bursts round them, then passes to whoever's farthest. Marked one stands apart. Rest keep off. When it jumps, the new one clears out. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A pale light marks one of us, which is the opposite of my profession. It bursts around them and passes to whoever is farthest away. The marked one stood apart; I kept off. It passed me by. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A pale grave-light on one of us, bursting around them and then passing to whoever was farthest away! I resented the competition. The marked one stood apart, the rest kept off, and the new one went away from everyone. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: a pale light on one of us, a burst around them, a pass to the farthest. Marked: stand apart. Others: keep off. Hot read: it jumped to me and I ran from everyone. Too far? No. Far enough. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "A pale light settled on one of us and burst around them and then jumped to whoever was farthest away, so the marked one ran off by themselves, and the rest kept off, and when it came to me I ran off round the edge, through nobody.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a pale light that bursts around one of us, then passes to the farthest. Remedy: marked, stand apart; others keep off; when it passes, move away from the rest. Note: each pass is on a beat. Label: infectious.",
      threadweaver: "A pale light marked one of my stitches and burst around them, then jumped to whoever stood farthest. The marked one drew the thread away from the rest, and we kept off. When it jumped, the new one did the same. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "A pale spotlight on one of the company: it bursts around the star, then passes to the actor farthest from them. The marked player stands apart, the rest keep off, and the spot moves on. (Exit the spotlight, pursued by manners.)",
      cleric: "A pale light marked one of my dears, burst around them, and passed to the farthest. The marked one stood apart, and the rest kept off, and when it came to them, they went off alone. I mended each before the burst. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The pale lantern marks one of my lot, bursts around them, and passes to whoever stands farthest. The marked one stands apart and the rest keep clear, like seed in a row. When it jumps to you, move away. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "A pale light marks one of us, and on the beat it passes to the farthest. The marked one stood apart, and the rest kept off. When it passed, the new one walked away. I'll have been the last one marked. I was.",
      tidecaller: "A pale light on one of us, bursting around them, then drifting to whoever stood farthest, like a lantern on a tide. The marked one stood apart, the rest kept off, and the new one went away from everyone. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "The Hollow King's lantern, hung on a branch. Its pale light marks one of us, bursts around them, then passes to whoever stands farthest. I kept apart from the others when it was mine. Three bursts, four of us, none caught. Walk where it's warm.",
      kenseier: "A pale light marks one of us, and the burst has a count. It passes to the farthest on the beat. The marked one stood apart, the rest kept off, and the next walked clear. A relay, not a duel. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Pale light on one of us, bursts round them, then jumps to whoever's farthest. Marked one stands apart. The rest of us kept off, hounds included. When it jumped to me I walked off alone. Heel.",
    } },
    'Nothing Left': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He drew in the whole grove, and the blow reached all of us. Nothing left to stand on, and no shield turns that. We had been too slow. Next time we break the limbs faster and finish him sooner. Gave ground.",
      gloomsworn: "He took everything: the grove, the floor, us. The watch ends when it ends, and we ran past it. We were too slow. I know when a post is lost. Next time, quicker work on the limbs.",
      thresher: "At the last he stops waiting, dear, and takes everything there is. Nothing is tidy after that. Be done before it, and break the limbs quickly. His name was almost off the list. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "He took everything at once and nobody got up from it! We were slow. We spent too long on the limbs. Be done first, my friend. Break them fast and hit him harder.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the grove goes quiet, and then Nothing Left. The answer: there is none, save to finish him before. Break the limbs without delay, and do not waste an opening. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "In the end my host ate the lot, the floor, the grove and us. We dawdled over the limbs, a poor diner's habit. Eat faster, break the limbs quicker, and finish him before the plates are cleared.",
      oathbroker: "The term expires: Nothing Left, at an hour fixed in advance. We did not complete performance, and the penalty is payable by all. The limbs were broken too slowly. Do not default again. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "He ran out of patience, and took everything, and none of us was left to answer. We were too slow with the limbs. Next time, quicker. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Whole grove goes. Nothing left. Too slow. Spent too long on the limbs. Break them fast, be done before. No line, no time.",
      shadowblade: "He took everything, the floor and the grove and all of us, and not even I could hide. We spent too long on the limbs. Be quick, be done, be gone. Rude of it to keep such strict time.",
      pyromancer: "Everything went! The floor, the grove, my eyebrows, all of us at once, and I had wasted time on the small limbs. Be quicker. Burn harder, break the limbs fast, and finish him first.",
      thermist: "He took everything: the floor, the grove, us. Too slow. We lingered on the limbs. Back to the middle: break them fast, burn him down early, and be done.",
      driftblade: "He took everything and there was nowhere to run, and I ran anyway, and it caught me, because we were slow with the limbs and Nothing Left does not wait, and every limb could have been fewer laps.",
      alembist: "Ailment: Nothing Left, at a fixed hour. Cause: slow work on the limbs. Remedy: break them early, hit hard, be done first. Shelf note: this brew spoils.",
      threadweaver: "He pulled every thread at once, and there was nothing left to hold. We'd been slow with the limbs. Next time, snip them early and finish him sooner. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "The final curtain fell on the wrong side, with the company still on stage. We lingered over the limbs. Next time, break them quickly and be done before the house lights go. (A bad review.)",
      cleric: "He took everything, and not one of us got up. We had been too slow with the limbs. I should have urged them harder. Forgive me, my dears. Next time, early.",
      grovekeeper: "He took the whole grove back, and us with it, like a late frost. We were slow with the limbs. Next time we prune quickly and finish early. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Nothing Left comes at the same hour however many times we undo it. We were slow with the limbs. Next time, which was last time, we break them faster, and finish early.",
      tidecaller: "He took everything, like the tide going out for good. We had been slow with the limbs. A short fight and a hard-hitting one, next time. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "Every light went out at once. We ran out of oil: too slow with the limbs. Next time, break them early and finish while the lamps are still warm. I'll leave one burning.",
      kenseier: "Nothing Left is a stroke without a count, and it comes at a fixed hour. We were late with the limbs. A master keeps time; next time, so do I. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Whole grove went, and us with it. Too slow with the limbs. Should've broken them quick and been finished first. Easy, lads.",
    } },
    'Twin Seed': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights, two seeds at once, joined by a cord. Burst far apart they open two small holes; too close, and they open one giant hole between them. Two of the line walked to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights, two seeds joined by a cord. Burst close together, they make one huge hole between them. So the two carry them to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. I have seen the inside of a big hole.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, two seeds, and a cord between them. Stand too near each other and they open one giant hole between you. So off the two go, to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. Do mind the cord.",
      bloodreaver: "Hard nights, two seeds tied together! Too close and they make a giant hole, a monstrous one. So the two carriers ran to opposite edges, far apart, and stepped out of the rings as the ground split. A doubly good one.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights two seeds are joined by a cord. The omen: they sit on two of us. The answer: carry them to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, else they open one giant hole between them. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights, two seeds, tied with string like a brace of sausages. Burst them close and they make one monstrous hole. We pulled them to opposite edges, as far apart as the table allows. A double helping, and twice as hollow.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms two seeds are bound by a cord. The clause: if the parties are close when they burst, one giant breach opens between them. The bearers therefore went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights, two seeds, joined. Close together, they speak as one, and the hole is huge. So the two went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and each opened its own. Two small words, not one great one. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: two seeds, joined by a cord. Burst them near each other, one giant hole between. Sent them to opposite edges, as far apart as possible. Clean line, by a mile.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the mark ties two seeds together. Too close, and they open one giant hole between them, which is greedy. The two went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and each stepped out in time. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Hard nights: two seeds on a cord, and if they burst close together they make a giant hole, a very rude one. The two ran to opposite edges, as far apart as they could get, and stepped out of their rings. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: two seeds on a cord. Close together, one giant hole between. Opposite edges: two small ones. Hot read: they sprinted apart, the cord pulling taut. Too close? Never. Back to the middle. Not that one.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights there are two seeds joined by a cord, and if they burst close together they open one giant hole, so the two ran out through everyone to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out as the ground split.",
      alembist: "Ailment: two seeds on a cord. Close together they open one giant hole. Remedy: opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, each stepping out as the ground splits. Note: the cord goes taut when it is far enough. Label: do not share a sample.",
      threadweaver: "Hard nights, two seeds tied on a cord. Too close and the thread bunches into one giant hole. So the two drew it taut, one to each edge, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out as the ground split. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights, a pair of seeds joined by a cord. Too close and they open one enormous trapdoor between them. The two players exited to opposite wings, as far apart as the stage allows. (Exeunt, severally.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights two seeds are bound by a cord. If their bearers stand close, one giant hole opens between them. So my two dears went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out in time. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "On hard nights two seeds in one furrow, tied by a cord. Sown too close, they'd make one great hole between them. So my two walked to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out as the ground split. Plant first, and wide.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the two seeds are tied together, and burst on the same beat. Too close and the hole is giant. So the two went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. I'll have said so earlier.",
      tidecaller: "Hard nights bring two seeds on one cord, like a pair of buoys. Too close, and they open one giant hole between them, a whirlpool. The two went to opposite shores, as far apart as the room allows. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights two seeds are tied together. If they burst close, one giant hole opens between. I walked with one, another with the other, to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows. Two lights, two edges. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "Two seeds on a cord. Both burst on the same beat, and if they are close, the hole between them is giant. The bearers went to opposite edges, as far apart as the room allows, and stepped out on the set. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, two seeds tied on a cord, like two dogs on one lead. Close together and they make a giant hole. Sent them to opposite edges, as far apart as the room goes. Heel.",
    } },
    'Hungering Dark': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights the ground around every hole leans in, and drags whoever stands on it over the edge. The ring is painted round each hole. The line kept outside every ring, and I kept the Oak clear of them. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in, and the ground round each of them drags whoever stands on it over the rim. The ring is painted. We kept outside every one of them. I would rather be buried than dragged.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, the holes breathe in, and the ground in a ring round each one drags whoever stands on it over the edge. The rings are painted, so stay outside them all. Such a hungry little gathering. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "The holes breathe in on the hard nights, and the ring of ground round each one drags whoever stands on it over the edge! The rings are painted. We stayed outside every one of them. I was tempted. A fine scar, avoided.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights the holes breathe in. The omen: a ring painted round each hole. The answer: the ground in the ring drags whoever stands on it into the hole, so we stand outside every ring. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights the holes inhale, and the ground in a ring round each one drags whoever stands on it over the rim. The rings are painted. I kept outside every one, which is hard for a man who loves a rim. Greedy, and rude.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms each hole draws in whoever stands in the painted ring about it. The clause: remain outside every ring. The fall is a heavy penalty, though not a default. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights the holes breathe in. The ground round each one is painted, and whoever stands in it is drawn over the edge. We stood outside every ring. The holes breathed in, and breathed out, and we did not. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: holes breathe in. Painted ring round each one. Stand in a ring, you're dragged over the rim. Stayed out of every ring. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in. A ring painted round each, and whoever stands in it is dragged over the edge, which is no way to treat a guest. I stood outside every ring. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The holes breathe in on the hard nights! The ground in a ring round each one drags whoever stands on it over the edge, and the rings glow the colour of trouble. I kept out of every one. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: the holes inhale. A ring painted round each one, and whoever stands in it is dragged over the rim. Stay outside them all. Hot read: I stood next to one, once. Too far in. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "On hard nights the holes breathe in and the ground in a ring round each one drags whoever stands on it over the edge, so I stayed out of every painted ring and ran the gaps between them, through the safe floor, never landing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the holes inhale; a ring of ground round each is painted, and whoever stands in it is dragged over the edge. Remedy: stay outside every ring. Note: holes at the rim leave more floor. Label: place wisely.",
      threadweaver: "Hard nights, the holes breathe in, and the ground in a ring round each pulls like a loose thread. Anyone on it is dragged over the edge. The rings are painted, and my stitches kept outside them all. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights the trapdoors breathe in, and the boards in a ring round each drag anyone on them over the edge. The rings are painted. The company kept outside every one. (Do not dance near a trapdoor.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in, and the ground in a ring round each one drags whoever stands on it over the edge. The rings are painted. I called my dears out of them. Thank the light for the paint.",
      grovekeeper: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in like a drain, and the ground in a ring round each pulls whoever stands on it over the edge. The rings are painted. My lot stayed outside every one. Plant first, and clear of the drain.",
      chronomancer: "The holes breathe in a beat after the rings are painted. Whoever stands in a ring is dragged over the edge. We stood outside every one before the breath. I'll have moved already.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights each hole breathes in like a whirlpool, and the ground round it drags whoever stands there over the edge. The rings are painted. We stood outside every one. The water gives back, the holes do not. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights the holes breathe in. A ring is painted round each, and whoever stands in it is dragged over the edge. I lit the safe ground outside the rings, and we stayed there. Four of us, every ring left empty. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "The holes breathe in on the hard nights, and the ground round each is painted and pulls with a count. Whoever stands in a ring is dragged over the edge. We stood outside every ring. Not a blow a blade can turn. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights the holes breathe in. Ring painted round each, and whoever's in it gets dragged over the rim. Kept clear of every ring, dogs too. Like a dog round a post. Heel.",
    } },
    'Deeper Roots': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights he rises with older limbs among the four: a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. Four are drawn from eight each time, and a different four each fight. We read which four before we chose the order. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights he grows his four limbs from eight, the old ones and older still: a wyrm's wing, a frost fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head join the cleaver, the roots, the storm and the lantern. Look before you break. I knew some of them dead.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, he draws his four limbs from eight: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. Different ones every time, so look first. Each has its own bar. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "Hard nights, four limbs picked from eight! The old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head, all from fights I loved. Different every pull. Look first, then break them in whatever order. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights he draws four limbs from eight: the old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head. The omen is which four grow, which differs every pull. Read them first, then choose the order. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights my host serves four limbs from a menu of eight, with the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune and the hydra's head among them. Different every time. Read the menu, then break them in the order you fancy.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, four limbs are drawn from eight, adding the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune and the hydra's head to the earlier four. The draw differs at each sitting. Read it first, then settle in the order preferred. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights he spoke with four voices drawn from eight: the old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head. The four change each time. We listened first, then chose the order. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: four limbs drawn from eight. The old four plus wyrm wing, frost fist, archmage rune, hydra head. Different four every pull. Read them first, then pick. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the mark grows four limbs, drawn from eight: the old ones, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. A different four every time, so look before choosing. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Hard nights: he grows four limbs from EIGHT, including a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head! A different four every pull, which is rude of him, so look first. Then choose the order. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: four limbs drawn from eight, the old four plus a wyrm's wing, a frost fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head. Different every pull. Read first. Hot read: break the worst first! Too hasty? Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights he grows four limbs drawn from eight, with a wyrm's wing and a frost giant's fist and an archmage's rune and a hydra's head among them, a different four every time, so I looked first and went through them in whatever order suited.",
      alembist: "Specimen: second form, hard pattern. Four limbs drawn from eight: the old four, plus wyrm wing, frost fist, archmage rune and hydra head. The draw changes every pull. Note: read the four before the first break. Label: keep the notes.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights he's sewn together from eight limbs and wears four: the old four, and the wyrm's wing, the frost giant's fist, the archmage's rune, the hydra's head. A different four every time. Check the seams first. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights the wardrobe holds eight costumes and he wears four: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head. A different four every night. (Check the programme.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights he grows four limbs drawn from eight, my dears, and older monsters have joined the lot: a wyrm, a frost giant, an archmage, a hydra. Never the same four twice. We looked first, then chose the order. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "On hard nights he grafts four limbs from a stock of eight. Beside the old four, a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. A different crop every pull. Look before you prune. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the limbs are drawn four from eight, so no pull is the last pull. The wyrm's wing, frost giant's fist, archmage's rune and hydra's head join the old four. Read them before the first break. I'll have read them already.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights he draws four limbs from eight, as a net draws fish: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune, a hydra's head. A different catch every pull. Read it first. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights he grows four limbs drawn from eight: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head. A different four every pull. I lit each of them to read it. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights he grows four limbs drawn from eight: the old four, and a wyrm's wing, a frost giant's fist, an archmage's rune and a hydra's head. Some of these I have crossed blades with. Read the four first. Breathe, then strike.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights: four limbs from eight. Old four, and wyrm's wing, frost giant's fist, archmage's rune, hydra's head. A different four each pull, so look first. Dogs knew every one of them. Good dogs.",
    } },
    'Wyrm Wing': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The wyrm's wing swept one whole half of the room, the half on the wing's side of him. The painted half told me which. The line went to the other half, and I held the Oak still for it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The wyrm's wing swept a whole half of the room, the side the wing grows on. The painted half is plain enough. We crossed to the other half. A wyrm and a tree is a tiring combination.",
      thresher: "The wyrm's wing, dear, sweeps a whole half of the room, the half the wing hangs on, painted so you can see. Cross to the other half, and hurry. Such a big wing. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "The wyrm's old wing swept half the room, the half it hangs on! The painted half was huge. We crossed to the other half. I missed the wyrm's fire, the real thing. A fine scar I did not take.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the wing stirs on its side, and half of the room is painted. The answer: the whole party crosses to the other half before it sweeps. The wyrm's wing, not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "The wyrm's wing, served with half the room, the half the wing hangs on, painted. We crossed to the other side. Scorched, and tough as a cheap cut.",
      oathbroker: "The wyrm's instrument is a wing, sweeping one half of the room, the half on its side, painted in advance. The undersigned removed to the opposite half. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The wyrm spoke through a wing: half the room, the half it hangs on, painted. We crossed to the other half and listened from there. It passed. Then we answered.",
      ranger: "Wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, painted, on the wing's side. Crossed to the other half. Clean line, if you cross early.",
      shadowblade: "The wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, the half it hangs on, painted. I crossed to the other half early, and was waiting when it passed. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The wyrm's wing! The old fire, half the room's worth, the half the wing hangs on, painted bright. We crossed to the other half, and I admired it from there. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: the wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, the half it hangs on, painted. Cross to the other half. Hot read: it was warm, almost home. Too close? Back to the middle, and across.",
      driftblade: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the half it hangs on, painted clear, so I ran through the middle and out into the other half and watched it sweep past behind me, never landing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, the side it hangs on, painted. Remedy: cross to the other half before it falls. Note: do it early, not late. Label: scorch.",
      threadweaver: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the side it hangs on, painted. My stitches crossed to the other half together, so nobody was left on the hem. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "The wyrm's wing sweeps half the stage, the half it hangs on, chalked clear. The whole company crossed to the other half, quickly. (Stage left! Stage left!)",
      cleric: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the half it hangs on, painted clear. I gathered my dears and crossed to the other half before it fell. Thank the light for the paint.",
      grovekeeper: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room like a hot wind, the half it hangs on, painted clear. We moved to the other half before the wind came. Plant first, and move early.",
      chronomancer: "The wing sweeps half the room a beat after the painting finishes. We crossed to the other half on the first beat. I will have been there already. I was.",
      tidecaller: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room like a wave breaking on one side, painted clear. We crossed to the other half, patient and early. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "The wyrm's wing swept half the room, the side it hangs on, painted clear. I lit the other half and we stood there before it fell. All four of us, the right half. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "The wyrm's wing sweeps half the room with a long count, the half it hangs on. A blade cannot turn a whole half. We crossed early to the other half. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Wyrm's wing sweeps half the room, the half it hangs on, painted. Crossed over to the other half, dogs too. Easy, lads.",
    } },
    'Frost Fist': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The frost giant's fist chose the one standing farthest from the Oak. A great ring followed that one, then set. They stepped out as it set, and the rest kept off it. The ice it left slowed anyone it touched, so we kept off that too. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The frost giant's fist picked whoever stood farthest from the Oak. A great ring followed that one, then set. They stepped out of it. Ice stayed behind and slowed whoever stood in it, so we kept off it too. Cold I can do. Slow I cannot.",
      thresher: "The frost giant's fist, dear, goes to whoever stands farthest from him, a great ring that follows them, then sets. Step out of it, everyone else keep clear, and mind the ice it leaves, which slows. Do mind the step.",
      bloodreaver: "The frost fist picked whoever stood farthest from the big one! A huge ring followed that one, then set. They stepped out, and the rest kept clear. The ice left behind slows anyone who stands in it, so we stayed off. A cold one.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a ring follows the one who stands farthest from him, then sets. The answer: they step out of it as it sets, the rest keep clear, and none of us stand in the ice that is left, for it slows. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The frost giant's fist, served cold to whoever sat farthest from the host. A huge ring followed that guest, then set. They stepped out, we kept clear, and nobody sat in the ice it left, which slows. Frozen, and flavourless.",
      oathbroker: "The frost giant's instrument is a fist, directed at the party standing farthest from the counterparty. A great ring follows them, then sets. They vacate it; the others stay clear; the ice left behind, which slows, is avoided. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The frost fist came for whoever stood farthest from the Oak. A great ring followed them, then settled. They stepped out of it, and we kept off. The ice stayed, and slowed. We went around. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Frost fist goes to whoever's farthest from him. Big ring follows, then sets. Stepped out. Rest kept clear. Ice left behind slows, stayed off it. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The frost giant's fist chooses whoever stands farthest from the mark, which is rarely me. A great ring follows them, then sets. They stepped out; we kept clear; nobody stood in the ice it left. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The frost giant's fist! It went for whoever stood farthest from him, a huge ring that followed them and then set. They stepped out, we kept clear, and I stayed well off the ice it left, which slows. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: the frost fist goes to the farthest from him; a ring follows, then sets. Step out. Keep clear of the ice it leaves, it slows. Hot read: I enjoyed it; I'm far too warm. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The frost giant's fist went for whoever was farthest from the Oak, a great ring that followed them and set, so they ran out of it through the others and everyone kept off the ice it left, which slows, and I especially.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the frost fist, on whoever stands farthest from him; a huge ring follows them, then sets. Remedy: they step out, the rest keep clear, nobody stands in the ice, which slows. Note: thaw takes a while. Label: cold.",
      threadweaver: "The frost fist chose whoever stood farthest from the Oak, a huge ring that followed them. They stepped out as it set, and my stitches kept clear. The ice it left slows, so we cut round it. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "The frost giant's fist: a great ring following the player farthest from the star. They stepped out as it set, the company kept clear, and nobody stood in the ice, which slows. (Exit, cold. Applause, stiff.)",
      cleric: "The frost fist chose whoever stood farthest from the Oak, a huge ring that followed them and set. They stepped out in time, the rest kept clear, and nobody stood in the ice, which slows. Dawn keep us all.",
      grovekeeper: "The frost giant's fist went to whoever stood farthest from the Oak, a huge ring that followed them and set like a late frost. They stepped out, we kept clear, and nobody stood in the ice left behind, which slows. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The fist follows whoever is farthest, then sets a beat later. They stepped out on the beat, the rest kept clear, and the ice it left, which slows, was left alone. I'll have said so, early.",
      tidecaller: "The frost giant's fist chose whoever stood farthest from the Oak, a huge ring, following, then setting, like ice forming on a pool. They stepped out; the rest kept clear; the ice left behind slows, so we stayed off. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "The frost giant's fist chose whoever stood farthest from the Oak. A huge ring followed them, then set. I lit their way out of it and we all kept clear. The ice it leaves slows anyone in it. We stayed on the warm side. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "The frost giant's fist goes to whoever stands farthest, and the ring follows, then sets on a count. They stepped out; we kept clear. The ice left behind slows, and a slow man misses his beat. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Frost fist went for whoever stood farthest from the old bull. Big ring followed him, then set. Stepped out. Kept the dogs off the ice, it slows. Sit. Good dogs.",
    } },
    'Archmage Rune': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A great rune circle opened at the Oak's feet, painted. We stood out of it until it burst, then stepped inside before the ring beyond it fell. Out, then in. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The archmage's rune opened at his feet: a circle first, then the ring outside it. Out of the circle until it burst, then into the middle before the ring. Out, then in. I did not expect to meet a wizard in a tree.",
      thresher: "The archmage's rune, dear, a great circle at his feet that bursts first, and then a ring outside it. So out of the circle, and when it bursts, in, before the ring falls. Out, then in. Off we go.",
      bloodreaver: "The archmage's glowing rune at the big one's feet! A circle that bursts first, then the ring outside it. Out until the circle goes, then in before the ring! Out, then in. A good one, if you're quick.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a rune circle at his feet, and a ring beyond it. The answer: stand outside the circle until it bursts, then step in before the ring does. Out, then in. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "The archmage's rune, a circle at my host's feet and then a ring outside it, like a plate and a rim. Out of the plate until it bursts, then in before the rim. A course in two parts. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "The archmage's rune: a circle is entered at his feet, then the ring outside it. The clause: stay outside the circle until it discharges, then step inside before the ring does. Out, then in. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The archmage's rune opened at his feet. A circle, then the ring outside it. A question, then an answer. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in before the ring. Out, then in. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Rune circle at his feet, then the ring beyond it. Out until the circle bursts. In before the ring. Clean line. Out, then in.",
      shadowblade: "The archmage's rune: a circle at the mark's feet, then the ring outside it. Out until the circle burst, then in at once, where nobody would think to look. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The archmage's rune! A glowing circle at his feet, then a ring outside it! I stood outside until the circle burst, then dashed inside before the ring fell. Out, then in. A beautiful bit of choreography. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: a rune circle at his feet, then a ring beyond it. Out of the circle until it bursts. Hot read: in, fast, before the ring falls. Out, then in. Too slow and I'd be toast. Back to the middle. Literally.",
      driftblade: "The archmage's rune opened a circle at his feet and then a ring outside it, so I ran around outside the circle until it burst and then through into the middle before the ring fell, out and then in, never landing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the archmage's rune: a circle at his feet, then the ring beyond it. Remedy: stand outside the circle until it bursts, then in before the ring falls. Out, then in. Note: the middle is safe the second time.",
      threadweaver: "The archmage's rune: a circle at his feet, then the ring outside it. My stitches stood outside the circle until it burst, then drew in tight before the ring fell. Out, then in. A neat bit of cutting. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "The archmage's rune: a spotlight at his feet, then the ring outside it. The company stood outside the spot until it blazed, then crossed in before the ring. (Out, then in. Never both.)",
      cleric: "The archmage's rune opened a great circle at his feet, and then a ring outside it. We stood outside the circle until it burst, then stepped in before the ring fell. Out, then in. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The archmage's rune, a circle at his feet and then a ring outside it, like a fairy ring. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in before the ring did. Out, then in. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The rune's circle bursts first, and a beat later the ring outside it. We waited outside the circle, then stepped in on the beat. Out, then in. I will have said it earlier. I did.",
      tidecaller: "The archmage's rune: a circle at his feet bursting, and a wave of ring outside it a few swells later. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in before the ring. Out, then in. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "The archmage's rune: a circle at his feet, and then a ring outside it. We stood outside the circle until it burst, then I lit the middle and we stepped in before the ring fell. Out, then in. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "The archmage's rune: two strokes, a circle and then the ring outside it. The first has a count; the second follows soon after. We stood out until the circle burst, then stepped in. Out, then in. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Archmage's rune at the old bull's feet: a circle first, then a ring outside it. Stood out till the circle went, then in before the ring, dogs too. Out, then in. Heel.",
    } },
    'Hydra Head': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The hydra's head took aim along a painted line from the limb to whoever stood farthest from it. That one stepped aside, and the line stayed on empty floor. The rest kept off it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The hydra's head painted a line from the limb to whoever stood farthest away. They stepped aside, and it fell on empty floor. The rest of us kept off the line. I have met a hydra; this one is less sociable.",
      thresher: "The hydra's head, dear, paints a line from the limb to whoever stands farthest from it. Step aside, and everyone keep off the line. Such a long reach. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "The hydra's head breathed a line at whoever stood farthest from it! It was painted first, so they stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line. I wanted it. Barely a scratch, avoided. Shameful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a line painted from the limb to the one who stands farthest from it. The answer: they step aside, and the rest keep off the line. The hydra's head, as foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The hydra's head, which breathes along a painted line at whoever sits farthest from it. That guest stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line. Wet, and a little reptilian.",
      oathbroker: "The hydra's head is directed along a painted line at the party farthest from it. That party stepped aside; the others kept off the line. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The hydra's head spoke along a painted line, to whoever stood farthest away. They stepped aside, and the word fell on empty floor. The rest of us kept off the line. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hydra head. Line painted to whoever's farthest. They stepped aside. Rest stayed off the line. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The hydra's head aimed a painted line at whoever was farthest from it. They stepped aside, the rest of us stood off the line, and it hit nothing. It never saw me, but it tried.",
      pyromancer: "The hydra's head! A line painted to whoever stood farthest from it, and then a breath down it. They stepped aside; we kept off the line. A far better breath than mine. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: the hydra's head paints a line to the farthest from it. Step aside. Everyone off the line. Hot read: the breath was warm. Not warm enough. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, so they stepped aside and I stayed off the line and ran round it, through the gap behind it, and the breath went past and hit only floor.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the hydra's head, a line painted to whoever stands farthest from it. Remedy: they step aside, and the rest stay off the line. Note: the line is painted before the breath. Label: reptile.",
      threadweaver: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, long and straight as a seam. They stepped aside, and the rest of us kept off the line. Mind the slack.",
      marionettist: "The hydra's head: a line painted to whoever stood farthest from it. They stepped aside, and the rest of the company kept off the line. (The spotlight missed. Booed.)",
      cleric: "The hydra's head breathed along a painted line at whoever stood farthest from it. They stepped aside, and the rest of us kept off the line. Dawn keep us, every head.",
      grovekeeper: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, like a creeper along a wall. They stepped aside; the rest of us kept off the line. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The line is painted first, and the breath follows a beat later along it. Whoever stood farthest stepped aside, and the rest kept off. I'll have moved first. I did.",
      tidecaller: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it, like a current finding a swimmer. They stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "The hydra's head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it. I hung a light on them and they stepped aside; the rest of us kept off the line. One light moved, none lost. Keep your light.",
      kenseier: "The hydra's head: a breath along a painted line, with a count. It goes to whoever stands farthest. They stepped aside, and the rest kept off the line. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Hydra head painted a line to whoever stood farthest from it. Stepped aside. Dogs and I kept off the line. Rust would've run straight down it, silly lad. Heel.",
    } },
    'Last Gasp': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one last blow, from where it fell, the same as before. I broke them, then held the ground and waited for the last one. It came. We moved. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights a broken limb does not stay broken. Its blow comes once more, from where it fell. I have been on the receiving end of last breaths. We moved as before.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, a limb that is broken still gets its last say: its move comes once more from the spot where it fell. Do mind it, and don't cheer too soon. Not yet!",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard nights a broken limb gets one more go, from where it fell! I like a limb with spirit. We dealt with it the same way as the first time, only more tired. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell. The omen: the same one as before. The answer: the same one. We were ready for it. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights, a limb struck from the menu is brought back once more, from where it fell, for one more course. The same dish, the same remedy. Reheated, and nothing new.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, a limb broken is not discharged. Its obligation falls due once more from where it fell. The remedy is the same as the first time. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights a broken limb speaks once more, from where it fell. The same word, the same answer. We did not forget. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: broken limb fires once more from where it fell. Same move, same answer. Don't relax when it drops. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires one last time from where it fell, which is rude of a corpse. Same move as before, same answer. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell! I could hardly believe the encore. Same move, same answer. I should not cheer too soon. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold read: a broken limb fires once more from where it fell. Same move, same answer. Hot read: I cheered too early. Too soon. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell, the very same move, so I broke it and kept running and was already through its path when it came, never landing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a broken limb, its blow repeated once from where it fell. Remedy: the same cure as the first time. Note: do not relax after breaking. Label: second dose.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one more go, from where it fell. The same move as before, so the same stitch in answer. Mind the slack, even after the snip.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights a broken limb takes a curtain call: its move fires once more, from where it fell. (Encore! The company knew the blocking.)",
      cleric: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one last blow from where it fell. The same one as before, so the same answer. I do not like a goodbye that bites. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "On the hard nights a cut limb still has one last season in it: its move comes again from where it fell. The same move, the same answer. Plant first, and don't relax at the pruning.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the broken limb fires once more from where it fell, a beat after it breaks. Same move, same answer. I'll have known it was coming. I did.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one more swell from where it fell. The same move as before, the same answer. The wave always breaks twice. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights a broken limb fires once more from where it fell. The same move as before, so I lit the same spots as before. A last flicker, then dark. I'll leave one burning.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights a broken limb gives one last stroke from where it fell. The same stroke, the same count, the same answer. A fallen blade still has an edge. Catch the beat.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, broken limb fires once more from where it fell. Same move, same answer. Don't call off the dogs till it's done. Heel.",
    } },
  },
};
